==English==
{{was wotd|2024|November|22}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*deh₁-|*leǵ-}}
{{lbor|en|grc|δέον|t=that which is binding, needful, proper, or right}} + [[w:English language|English]] {{affix|en|-ology}} (a variant of {{m|en|-logy|pos={{glossary|suffix}} denoting a branch of learning or a study of a particular subject}}). {{m|grc||Δέον}} is the {{glossary|neuter}} {{glossary|present}} {{glossary|participle}} of {{m|grc|δεῖ|t=it behoves one to, it is necessary to, one must}},<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=n|id=1582219015|date=July 2023}}{{R:Lexico}}</ref> from {{m|grc|δέω|t=to bind, tie; to fasten; to fetter}}, ultimately from {{der|en|ine-pro|*deh₁-|t=to bind}}.

{{senseno|en|ethics|uc=1}} (“''synonym of'' ethics”) was {{coinage|en|Jeremy Bentham|nat=the English|occ=philosopher|nocap=1}} (1748–1832) to refer to [[censorial]] or [[dicastic]] ethics (ethics based on judgment),<ref>{{cite-book|author=w:Jeremy Bentham|chapter=Deontology Explained—Term Why Adopted|editor=w:John Bowring|title=Deontology; or, The Science of Morality:{{nb...|In which the Harmony and Co-incidence of Duty and Self-interest, Virtue and Felicity, Prudence and Benevolence, are Explained, Exemplified, and Applied to the Business of Life. From the MSS. of Jeremy Bentham. Arranged and Edited by John Bowring. In Two Volumes.}}|location=London|publisher=[[w:Longman|Longman, Rees, Orme, Browne, Green, and Longman]]; Edinburgh: [[w:William Tait (publisher)|William Tait]]|year=written ''{{glossary|a.}}'' 1833|year_published=1834|volume=I|section=part I (Theory of Virtue)|page=21|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/deontologyorscie01bent/page/21/mode/1up|oclc=946613781|passage={{smallcaps|Deontology}} is derived from the Greek words, {{lang|grc|το δεον}} (that which is proper) and {{lang|grc|λογια}}, knowledge—meaning the knowledge of what is right or proper; and it is here specifically applied to the subject of morals, or that part of the field of action which is not the object of public legislation. As an art, it is the doing what is fit to be done; as a science, the knowing what is fit to be done on every occasion.}}</ref> while {{senseno|en|normative theory}} (“normative ethical theory that the morality of an action should be based on whether the action follows certain obligations or rules”) derives from the use of the word {{m|en|deontological}} by the English philosopher [[w:C. D. Broad|Charlie Dunbar Broad]] (1887–1971) in his book ''Five Types of Ethical Theory'' (1930).<ref>{{cite-book|en|author=[[w:C. D. Broad|C[harle] D[unbar] Broad]]|chapter=[[w:Henry Sidgwick|Sidgwick]]|title=Five Types of Ethical Theory|location=London|publisher=[[w:Routledge|Routledge & Kegan Paul]]{{nb...|Ltd, Broadway House: 68–74 Carter Lane, E.C.4}}|year=1930|year_published=1950|page=162|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/dli.ernet.425387/page/162/mode/1up|oclc=6947868|passage=There is another distinction to be drawn between what I call the ''deontological'', the ''teleological'', and the ''logical'' application of “ought”. Some people judge that there are certain types of action which ought to be done (or avoided) in all or in certain types of situation, regardless of the goodness or badness of the probable consequences. This is what I call the “deontological” application of “ought”.}}</ref>

