===Noun===
{{en-noun|-|+}} {{term-label|en|ethics}}

# {{senseid|en|ethics}}{{synonym of|en|ethics|t=the [[study#Noun|study]] of [[principle#Noun|principles]] [[relate|relating]] to [[right#Adjective|right]] and [[wrong#Adjective|wrong]] [[conduct#Noun|conduct]]}} {{defdate|from c. 1826}}
#: {{synonyms|en|moral philosophy}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1826|month=October|author=w:Jeremy Bentham|title=Art. VIII.—''Observations on the Actual State of the English Law of Real Property, with the Outlines of a Code.'' By [[w:James Humphreys (lawyer)|James Humphreys]], Esq. of Lincoln’s Inn, Barrister. 8vo. Murray. London. [book review]|journal=w:The Westminster Review|location=London|publisher=Baldwin, Cradock, and Joy|volume=VI|issue=XII|page=448|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sim_westminster-review_1826-10_6/page/448/mode/1up|oclc=311488514|passage={{quote-gloss|{{w|Matthew Hale}}}} was no reformer: nothing better than an expounder: every thing stated by him was stated as he found it, or conceived it to be: no inquiries as to what it ought to be: in the eyes of lawyers—not to speak of the dupes—that is to say, as yet, the generality of non-lawyers—the ''is'' and the ''ought to be'' (or, as in Greek it would be so much better—the {{lang|grc|το ον}} and the {{lang|grc|το δεον}}, from which last, Ethics has received the more expressive name of '''''Deontology'''''), were one and indivisible.}}
#* {{RQ:Bentham Deontology|volume=I|chapter=Deontology Explained—Term Why Adopted|pages=23–24|pageref=23|passage=The principle, then, on which '''Deontology''' is grounded, is the principle of ''Utility''; in other words, that every action is right or wrong—worthy or unworthy—deserving approbation or disapprobation, in proportion to its tendency to contribute to, or to diminish the amount of public happiness.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1898|author=[John Martin Russell]|chapter=&#91;{{w|Book of Revelation}}.&#93; Chapter XIII, 11–18. The Second Beast.|title=The Seven Ages of Creation: Or Cosmos and the Mysteries Expounded{{nb...|Written by a Voice in the West and Dedicated to the Spirit of Truth}}|location=Fort Dodge, Iowa|publisher=E. A. Tryon,{{nb...|printer and binder}}|page=231|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/sevenagesofcreat00russ/page/231/mode/1up|oclc=16931247|passage=And then she discoursed with voluble blandiloquence on '''deontologies''' and morals, and pondered with imposing gravity on ancient lores, and wisdoms, and beatifications, and in all her countenance seemeth as demure as a babe asleep.}}
# {{senseid|en|normative theory}}{{lb|en|specifically}} The [[normative ethics|normative ethical]] [[theory]] that the [[morality]] of an [[action#Noun|action]] should be [[base#Verb|based]] on whether the action [[follow#Verb|follows]] certain [[obligation]]s or [[rule#Noun|rules]], rather than on either its [[inherent]] [[goodness#Noun|goodness]] or its [[consequence]]s. {{defdate|from 1930}}

